xnu 0191: a GPU that stops answering is marked stopped and left alone

HackiOS does this in drivers/gpu/hakios.adreno.c: a fence that is not answered
means the GPU is reported, marked stopped and never touched again, and the
system keeps running. This port kept submitting, and the next submission writes
CP_RB_WPTR into a GPU whose bus is stalled -- an access that never returns and
takes the CPU with it, so the reset thread never runs and the phone has to be
power-cycled by hand.

The measured sequence is exactly that: the first stream's fence arrives (71
dwords, 12 ms), the second one does not (168 dwords, done 0), and the boot dies
shortly after. Refusing submissions once a fence goes unanswered keeps the
system alive (and the ring readable) when the CP stops.

--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_gpu.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_gpu.c
@@ -3112,6 +3112,18 @@
 		printf("MONDRIAN GPU: uc big %u dwords fence %u done %u in %u ms\n",
 		    dwords, seq, (int32_t)(value - seq) >= 0, waited);
 		mondrian_log_flush_now();
+		if ((int32_t)(value - seq) < 0) {
+			/*
+			 * The fence was not answered: the GPU has stopped
+			 * fetching and every later register access is likely
+			 * to hang the CPU with it (that is what killed the
+			 * boots). HackiOS reports the GPU and leaves it
+			 * stopped; refuse submissions instead of touching it.
+			 */
+			printf("MONDRIAN GPU: gpu stopped answering, submissions refused\n");
+			mondrian_log_flush_now();
+			mondrian_uc_up = 0;
+		}
 		if (mondrian_uc_dump != 0) {
 			/* An opcode error names the packet the ucode would not
 			 * take: SQE_STAT index 0 is the ucode PC, index 1 the
